    ],
    "text": "Arrowsrepresent direction of flow.",
    "source_type": "text"
  },
  {
    "layout_id": "page-14:figure-5",
    "page_id": 14,
    "layout_type": "figure",
    "asset_ref": "assets/_page_14_Figure_0.jpg",
    "bbox_norm": [
      0.05,
      0.399,
      0.873,
      0.873
    ],
    "text": "",
    "source_type": "image"
  },
  {
    "layout_id": "page-15:text-1",
    "page_id": 15,
    "layout_type": "text",
    "asset_ref": null,
    "bbox_norm": [
      0.034,
      0.027,
      0.111,
      0.587
    ],
    "text": "Electronic control unit",
    "source_type": "text"
  },
  {
    "layout_id": "page-15:text-2",
    "page_id": 15,
    "layout_type": "text",
    "asset_ref": null,
    "bbox_norm": [
      0.154,
      0.002,
      0.395,
      0.996
    ],
    "text": "· In automotive electronics, electronic control unit (ECU) is a generic term for any embedded system that controls one or more of the electrical systems or subsystems in a motor vehicle.",
    "source_type": "text"
  },
  {
    "layout_id": "page-15:text-3",
    "page_id": 15,
    "layout_type": "text",
    "asset_ref": null,
    "bbox_norm": [
      0.416,
      0.002,
      0.913,
      0.997
    ],
    "text": "· An engine control unit (ECU), also known as power-train control module (PCM), or engine control module (ECM) is a type of electronic control unit that determines the amount of fuel, ignition timing and other parameters an internal combustion engine needs to keep running. It does this by reading values from multidimensional maps which contain values calculated by sensor devices monitoring the engine.",
    "source_type": "text"
  },
  {
    "layout_id": "page-16:text-1",
    "page_id": 16,
    "layout_type": "text",
    "asset_ref": null,
    "bbox_norm": [
      0.053,
      0.032,
      0.137,
      0.44
    ],
    "text": "Working of ECU",
    "source_type": "text"
  },
  {
    "layout_id": "page-16:text-2",
    "page_id": 16,
    "layout_type": "text",
    "asset_ref": null,
    "bbox_norm": [
      0.164,
      0.002,
      0.569,
      0.998
    ],
    "text": "Control of fuel injection: ECU willdetermine the quantity of fuel to inject based on a number of parameters. If the throttle pedal is pressed further down, this will open the throttle body and allow more air to be pulled into the engine. The ECU will inject more fuel according to how much air is passing into the engine. If the engine has not warmed up yet, more fuel will be injected .",
    "source_type": "text"
  },
  {
    "layout_id": "page-16:text-3",
    "page_id": 16,
    "layout_type": "text",
    "asset_ref": null,
    "bbox_norm": [
      0.579,
      0.003,
      0.878,
      0.996
    ],
    "text": "Control of ignition timing : A spark ignition engine requires a spark to initiate combustion in the combustion chamber. An ECU can adjust the exact timing of the spark (called ignition timing) to provide better power and economy.",
    "source_type": "text"
  },
  {
    "layout_id": "page-17:text-1",
    "page_id": 17,
    "layout_type": "text",
    "asset_ref": null,
    "bbox_norm": [
      0.013,
      0.0,
      0.463,
      0.998
    ],
    "text": "· Control of idle speed : Most engine systems have idle speed control built into the ECU. The engine RPM is monitored by the crankshaft position sensor which plays a primary role in the engine timing functions for fuel injection，spark events,and valve timing. Idle speed is controlled by a programmable throtte stop or an idle air bypass control stepper motor.",
    "source_type": "text"
  },
  {
    "layout_id": "page-17:figure-2",
    "page_id": 17,
    "layout_type": "figure",
    "asset_ref": "assets/_page_17_Figure_0.jpg",
    "bbox_norm": [
